<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Entrepreneurial Spirit – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Entrepreneurial Spirit</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How to Market a Business Idea</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Give a speech on an academic topic. Interactive: Collaborate in developing plans for group activities.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can give a speech on innovations, providing examples to support ideas (e.g., "Businesses have started using renewable energy to cut costs and attract eco-conscious customers.")
            Can collaborate on planning a business idea to discuss potential outcomes (e.g., "If we advertise on social media, we might reach more customers.")
            Can lead discussions to share plans for ideas and evaluate others' ideas (e.g., "I think launching an app could enhance customer engagement.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays images of innovative products or services (e.g., a smart home device, an eco-friendly food delivery service, a language learning app). Students work in pairs to discuss: "If you had to market one of these ideas, what is the first thing you would do to make it visible to customers? Why?" (2-3 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models phrases for sharing initial ideas and discussing potential outcomes. For example: "I think if we launch an app, we might increase customer engagement significantly." and "Local businesses have successfully used social media to promote their products." The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of words like "launch" /lɔːntʃ/, "cost" /kɔːst/, "talk" /tɔːk/, and highlights the stressed syllables and reduced vowels in multisyllabic words like "renewable" /rɪˈnuː.ə.bəl/ and "successful" /səkˈses.fəl/ during these examples.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say, 'If we advertise, we might reach more people,' am I talking about something that is certain or a possibility?" (Possibility)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does 'customer engagement' mean? Is it when customers buy something, or when they interact with the business?" (Interact)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Entrepreneurial Spirit".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short audio dialogue (2-3 minutes) between two young entrepreneurs, Ana and Ben, discussing marketing strategies for their new eco-friendly product. The dialogue features the target grammar (First Conditional, Present Perfect for innovations, Future Continuous) and vocabulary (e.g., *innovation, strategy, customer base, app, promotional events, visibility, collaborate, market, advertise*).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Understanding and producing oral texts to collaborate in business contexts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> "What is the main challenge Ana and Ben are discussing?" (Marketing their new product effectively.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students listen and decide if statements are true or false based on the dialogue. E.g., "Ana suggests hosting a promotional event." (T) "Ben thinks they have already increased their customer base significantly through social media." (F)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match phrases from the dialogue to their speaker (Ana or Ben). E.g., "If we launch an app..." (Ana) "We've implemented a new digital strategy..." (Ben) "I'll be analyzing market trends tomorrow." (Ana)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0;">7-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Practicing First Conditional (If + Present Simple, will/might/could + Base Verb), Present Perfect for innovations (Subject + have/has + Past Participle), Future Continuous (Subject + will be + Verb-ing). Target vocabulary related to marketing and business.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice - Sentence Completion (Pair Work):</b> Teacher provides sentence starters on a handout. Students work in pairs to complete them using the target grammar and vocabulary. E.g., "If we ______ (verb: automate) our customer service, we ______ (verb: improve) efficiency." "Our company ______ (Present Perfect of implement) several innovative strategies to ______ (verb: enhance) engagement." "Next month, I ______ (Future Continuous of analyze) consumer demand." Students read their completed sentences aloud to their partner, focusing on clear pronunciation of /ɔː/ words and reduced vowels in multisyllabic words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice - Guided Dialogue (Pair Work):</b> Students are given roles (e.g., "Startup Founder" and "Marketing Consultant") and scenario cards (e.g., "Problem: Low brand visibility," "Goal: Increase customer base"). They must ask and answer questions using specific prompts. For example: "What strategies <b>have</b> we <b>implemented</b> so far?" (Consultant) "We <b>have developed</b> a unique product line, but we haven't promoted it enough." (Founder) "If we <b>host promotional events</b>, we <b>might increase</b> our visibility. I'll <b>be researching</b> local venues next week." (Consultant) The teacher circulates, providing feedback on grammar and pronunciation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction - Idea Exchange & Peer Check:</b> Students are presented with a hypothetical small business (e.g., a local bakery wanting to expand or a small tech repair shop struggling to attract new customers). In pairs, they brainstorm 3-4 marketing ideas. They must use first conditional to discuss the potential outcomes of each idea (e.g., "If we offer online ordering, we could reach a wider customer base.") and mention one innovation already observed in similar businesses using the present perfect (e.g., "Other bakeries have started using apps for delivery."). They give constructive feedback to each other on their ideas and the use of the target language, showing respect for innovative suggestions.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Entrepreneurial Spirit".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group Pitch - "Market My Innovation"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in groups of 3-4. Each group invents an innovative business idea (e.g., a sustainable fashion rental service, an AI-powered tutoring platform, a customizable healthy snack delivery).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Their task is to prepare a short, engaging marketing pitch (3-4 minutes) to "investors" (the teacher and other classmates).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The pitch must include:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A description of their innovation and target customer base.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">At least two examples of how similar businesses <b>have implemented</b> successful strategies or innovations (Present Perfect).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Three to four specific marketing strategies they plan to use, clearly explaining potential outcomes using the <b>first conditional</b> (e.g., "If we advertise on social media, we might reach a much larger audience and increase brand visibility.").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One sentence explaining what they <b>will be doing</b> next to develop their plan (Future Continuous).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of professional language for business ideas (e.g., "This strategy could attract more customers to our project.").</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups present their pitches. After each pitch, the "investors" (classmates) can ask one question about their strategy. Students must lead discussions to share plans and evaluate others' ideas.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0;">12-15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on the use of target grammar and vocabulary, clarity, fluency, and collaboration. Peer assessment rubric for group presentations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students deliver their marketing pitch and respond to questions, demonstrating their ability to give a speech on an academic topic and collaborate in developing plans.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist & Peer Rubric):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation (especially /ɔː/ and reduced vowels in multisyllabic words).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of target vocabulary (e.g., *innovation, strategy, customer base, launch, enhance*).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate use of First Conditional to express potential outcomes.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate use of Present Perfect for innovations.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate use of Future Continuous to describe next steps.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and coherence of the business idea and marketing strategies.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and engagement with group members and audience (showing respect for suggestions).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of professional language for business ideas.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick reflection:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new marketing strategy or vocabulary word you learned today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How well did your group collaborate to create the marketing pitch?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of developing and presenting your business idea did you find most challenging, and why?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do you feel now using the first conditional to talk about future business outcomes?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The pitches were lively and students demonstrated good interaction. Most groups successfully integrated the first conditional and relevant vocabulary. Some struggled with consistent use of the present perfect for innovations; more focused practice might be needed in future lessons or a quick review next time. Pronunciation of /ɔː/ sounds was generally good, but reduced vowels in longer words will require continuous reinforcement. What adjustments and scaffolds are needed for next lesson? Provide sentence starters or a word bank for the next writing task to ensure accurate grammar usage, specifically targeting the structures practiced today.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> In Lesson 4 (Writing Composition), students will write a short business proposal or an executive summary for their innovative business idea, incorporating the marketing strategies discussed and the grammar structures practiced today.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>